---
/*
 * /methodology/ - institutional Methodology page.
 *
 * Source content: editorial/drafts/methodology_page.md (style-editor pass
 * complete, TK-free, ~590 words across four H2 sections). The body is
 * rendered inline as HTML rather than routed through Astro's markdown loader
 * for the same reason as /about/: a single page with no per-edit churn does
 * not need the indirection. The draft remains the source of truth.
 *
 * Visual register: Vignelli, mirroring /about/. Shared `.static` +
 * `.markdown-body` surface (defined inline below; identical to /about/ so the
 * two pages render as a matched pair). H2 sections carry the canonical
 * 1px hairline underline.
 */

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../data/sections";

const pageTitle = "Methodology";
const pageDesc =
  "How Sibley Creek does its research: simple methods, specific cases, " +
  "and the people closest to the story.";
---
<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDesc} pageClass="static-page">
  <article class="static" aria-labelledby="static-h">
    <header class="static__header">
      <h1 id="static-h" class="static__h">Methodology</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="static__body markdown-body">
      <p>
        Sibley Creek is client-led. A reporter, a subscriber, or a
        client brings the question; we go and answer it. The work that
        follows leans on three principles.
      </p>

      <h2>Simplicity</h2>

      <p>
        We use the simplest method that answers the question. Averages,
        medians, ranges, contributions analysis, well-chosen charts. The
        bar for adding complexity is high. A linear regression earns its
        place when a chart and a table cannot get there; a vector
        autoregression has to clear a higher bar still. Methods we
        cannot fully explain to a client do not ship.
      </p>

      <h2>Specificity</h2>

      <p>
        Aggregates are where a question starts, not where it ends. A
        piece on Canadian housing should name specific cities, specific
        developments, specific buyers. A piece on inflation should name
        the products that moved and what they cost. The point of a
        national average is to surface the question; the answer lives
        one or two layers below.
      </p>

      <h2>Triangulation</h2>

      <p>
        Data analysis is one leg of the work. The other is talking to
        the people closest to the process being studied: the broker who
        works a regional market, the procurement manager who reprices
        the input, the official who implemented the policy. Conventional
        experts aggregate and synthesize; ground-level sources see what
        the aggregates smooth out. The best work combines both.
      </p>

      <p>
        In practice this means: start with the data, form a tentative
        read, stress-test that read against people doing the work, and
        sharpen the answer in light of what they say. Research is
        published when both legs agree, or when the disagreement itself
        is the story.
      </p>

      <h2>Primary sources</h2>

      <p>
        The data spine is Statistics Canada, the Bank of Canada, the
        Department of Finance, CMHC, and CREA, with selected market
        feeds for prices and yields. Each section of the live tracker
        refreshes when its source releases, and revisions flow through
        on the next build. For a full source list and the team behind
        the work, see <a href="/about/">the about page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    /* Shared static-page surface; mirrors /about/. See about.astro for the
     * design-system citation. Kept inline (not extracted to a shared
     * component) because the two pages are the only consumers and the
     * markup is mostly content, not structure. */

    /* Boutique register — matches /about/, /work-with-us/, /contact/. */
    .static {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 720px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 32px 24px 64px;
    }

    .static__header {
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
      padding-bottom: 24px;
      margin-bottom: 32px;
    }
    .static__kicker {
      margin: 0 0 12px;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 13px;
      line-height: 1;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink);
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    .static__h {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 42px;
      line-height: 1.1;
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    .static__body {
      /* No top margin: header already has margin-bottom: 32px. */
    }

    .markdown-body h2 {
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 14px;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink);
      margin: 40px 0 16px;
      padding-bottom: 8px;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
    }
    .markdown-body h2:first-child {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .markdown-body p {
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-weight: 400;
      font-size: 18px;
      line-height: 1.55;
      color: var(--ink);
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }
    .markdown-body p:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    .markdown-body strong,
    .markdown-body b {
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    .markdown-body em,
    .markdown-body i {
      font-style: normal;
      font-weight: 200;
    }

    .markdown-body a {
      color: var(--ink);
      text-decoration-color: var(--ink);
    }
    .markdown-body a:hover {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-decoration-color: var(--accent);
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
